<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1990">1990</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rashid bin Saeed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maktoum">Al Maktoum</a> was ruler of Dubai from 1958 to 1990 and the main builder of the modern city. He dredged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubai-creek">Dubai Creek</a>, opened an airport in 1960 and used the oil revenue that followed the offshore discoveries of 1966 to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-rashid">Port Rashid</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-ali">Jebel Ali</a> port. He was a founder of the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> and its vice-president and prime minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashid-bin-saeed-al-maktoum.jpg" alt="Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:His_Highness_Rashid_bin_Saeed_Al_Maktoum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum succeeded as ruler of Dubai on the death of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeed-bin-maktoum-al-maktoum">Saeed bin Maktoum Al Maktoum</a>, on 10 September 1958. He had effectively managed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s affairs since the late 1930s, having refused under considerable British pressure to force his father to abdicate. By the 1950s Dubai's port was the most important in the Gulf, and its merchants grew rich supplying gold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, shipped by air from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and carried by dhow to the edge of Indian waters.</p><p>Dubai's creek was silting up. A plan drawn up by the British consultancy Halcrow in 1954 led to dredging between 1958 and 1959, paid for partly by creek bonds and a loan of £500,000 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwait">Kuwait</a>, so that larger ships could enter. Rashid also commissioned a town plan, founded a municipal council in 1957, started water and electricity companies and had Maktoum Bridge, the first crossing of the creek, built in 1963 with a loan from his son-in-law, the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Dubai found oil in 1966 at the Fateh field, 60 miles offshore. Raising credit against the expected revenue, Rashid ordered a deep-sea container terminal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-rashid">Port Rashid</a>, which he enlarged from the planned four berths to 21 and which opened in 1972. Dubai's population grew from 59,000 in 1968 to over 278,000 in 1980. In 1960 he had pressed the British for an airstrip, and an asphalt runway for jetliners followed in May 1965. Jebel Ali Port, with 66 berths, opened in 1983.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> announced in January 1968 that it would end its protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>, Rashid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Sheikh Zayed</a> bin Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a> of Abu Dhabi first urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to reconsider. On 18 February 1968 the two men met in a tent in the desert near Semeih and agreed the principle of a union with a handshake. The United Arab Emirates was declared on 18 July 1971, with Rashid as vice president. He negotiated parity with Abu Dhabi in the Federal National Council and, with Abu Dhabi, a veto in the Supreme Council, and he became prime minister on 30 April 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rashid died on 7 October 1990 after almost a decade of illness. His son Maktoum bin Rashid Al Maktoum, prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a> from 1971 to 1979, succeeded him as ruler of Dubai, and another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-bin-rashid-al-maktoum">Mohammed bin Rashid Al Maktoum</a>, later followed Maktoum. Mohammed had been given charge of Dubai's airport and civil defence in the late 1960s and developed its aviation and tourism with his father's approval.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A month later Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan, ruler of Abu Dhabi since 1966, and <b>Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum</b> of Dubai agreed at a meeting in the desert between their two emirates to form a union and invited the other rulers to join them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum</b> dredged the creek and opened an airport in 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-dubai">Sheikhdom of Dubai</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dubai, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-arab-emirates">the United Arab Emirates</a>. Died in Dubai, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-arab-emirates">the United Arab Emirates</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.27,55.31&amp;year=1962" aria-label="Dubai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6016 1234 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6466 1504h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6466 1504h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.27,55.31&amp;year=1962">What was here in 1962? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
